-exits `9`, before either `--check` or `--write` ever runs.
+`fmt` does not trust its own output blindly — but it no longer checks that
+on every run, either. Through 1.3 `cmd_fmt` re-parsed the canonical text it
+had just written and refused to emit anything whose records or labels moved
+in the trip, at the cost of a full second parse on every invocation. That
+guard could only ever catch a defect in `write_surface` itself — never
+something a user's input could trigger — so no document a person could
+actually format would reach it, and no test could distinguish a guard that
+checked from one that did not.
+
+The property it protected is asserted where it can actually be exercised:
+`every_accepted_mutation_round_trips` in
+`crates/smysl-core/tests/surface.rs` fuzzes thousands of mutated documents
+and asserts that `write_surface`'s output re-parses to the same records,
+and `crates/smysl-core/tests/versioning.rs` asserts a document round-trips
+declaring the version it declared. Those run once per test suite instead of
+once per file `fmt` ever touches.
 
 #callout(label: "Why")[
-  This assertion is not about your document — it is `fmt` checking
-  *itself*, on every single run, rather than trusting once during
-  development that the writer and the parser agree. If a future change to
-  the writer ever produced text the parser would read back differently,
-  this is the guard that would catch it immediately, on the very first
-  file anyone ran it against, rather than as a silent corruption discovered
-  much later.
+  A per-run assertion earns its keep by catching something a test suite
+  cannot. This one could not: the only way to make it fire was to break
+  `write_surface` in a way that would also fail the property tests above,
+  so paying for it on every invocation bought no defense a `cargo test` run
+  did not already provide. Moving the check to test time keeps the
+  guarantee and drops the cost.
 ]
 
